        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Multiplies two arrays and adds up over a number of matching axes: the last axes of the first array against the first axes of the second.\n\nWith axes 1 it is the matrix product (np.matmul) - each row of the first against each column of the second - extended to arrays of any number of dimensions: a 3-D array of frames x joints x xyz times a 3 x 3 rotation transforms every point of every frame at once. With axes 2 it multiplies the last two axes of the first by the first two of the second, element by element, and adds everything up; for two matrices that gives a single number measuring how alike they are. With axes 0 it is the outer product.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.tensordot <axes: int>\nDefault: axes 2 (NumPy's default).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.tensordot 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe first array. Every input is multiplied and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe second array, held: send it first.\n\naxes (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many axes to sum over. The sizes of the last that many axes of in must equal the first that many of in 2.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe product. Its axes are in's remaining axes followed by in 2's.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA is 1 2 / 3 4; B is 0 1 / 1 0, the matrix that swaps columns. With axes 1, A times B is 2 1 / 4 3: A with its columns swapped. With axes 2, every element of A times the matching one of B, all added: 0 + 2 + 3 + 0 = 5. A metro re-sends A five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.matmul and np.dot are the axes 1 case for matrices. np.einsum can say exactly which axes to sum. np.outer is the axes 0 case for lists.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Multiplies two arrays and adds up over a number of matching axes: the last axes of the first array against the first axes of the second.\n\nWith axes 1 it is the matrix product (np.matmul) - each row of the first against each column of the second - extended to arrays of any number of dimensions: a 3-D array of frames x joints x xyz times a 3 x 3 rotation transforms every point of every frame at once. With axes 2 it multiplies the last two axes of the first by the first two of the second, element by element, and adds everything up; for two matrices that gives a single number measuring how alike they are. With axes 0 it is the outer product.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.tensordot <axes: int>\nDefault: axes 2 (NumPy's default).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.tensordot 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe first array. Every input is multiplied and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe second array, held: send it first.\n\naxes (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many axes to sum over. The sizes of the last that many axes of in must equal the first that many of in 2.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe product. Its axes are in's remaining axes followed by in 2's.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA is 1 2 / 3 4; B is 0 1 / 1 0, the matrix that swaps columns. With axes 1, A times B is 2 1 / 4 3: A with its columns swapped. With axes 2, every element of A times the matching one of B, all added: 0 + 2 + 3 + 0 = 5. A metro re-sends A five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.matmul and np.dot are the axes 1 case for matrices. np.einsum can say exactly which axes to sum. np.outer is the axes 0 case for lists."
        },
